.TH GIT-WT 1 "2026-07-29" "gitplus" "gitplus Manual"
.SH NAME
git-wt \- print the worktree path that already has a branch checked out
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git wt
.I BRANCH
.br
.B git wt
[\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A branch can only ever be checked out in one worktree at a time (a hard git
rule). Given
.IR BRANCH ,
.B git wt
prints the path of the worktree that already has it: the current worktree's
path if you're already on
.IR BRANCH ,
another worktree's path if it's checked out there, or a clear error
(exit 1) if it isn't checked out anywhere at all. It doesn't check anything
out or create anything.
.PP
Paired with the
.B gwt
shell function for an actual instant
.BR cd :
a plain script can never change its parent shell's directory, so
.B git wt
only resolves the path \(em
.B gwt
does the
.BR cd .
See
.I share/zsh/gwt.zsh
in the gitplus source; source it from
.I ~/.zshrc
to get the function.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B gwt spec-first
Jump straight to whichever worktree has
.I spec-first
checked out.
.TP
.B cd "$(git wt spec-first)"
The same thing, spelled out without the shell function.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-sync (1),
.BR git-pr (1),
.BR git-worktree (1)
